1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,000
A bigger claim than the last episode: real Python, the actual language, running inside the browser tab, executing a genuine Python tool with no server anywhere in the loop.

2
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:12,000
The desktop app runs Python tools by shelling out to the system Python on your PATH.

3
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:20,000
A browser tab can't shell out to anything — so pyodide dot ts swaps in Pyodide: an actual build of CPython, compiled to WebAssembly, that runs entirely inside the page.

4
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:21,390
It isn't bundled with the app.

5
00:00:21,390 --> 00:00:28,062
The first time any Python tool actually runs, pyodide dot ts pulls the runtime from the official CDN — a few megabytes, once, cached after that.

6
00:00:28,062 --> 00:00:32,000
Every other user of the web build who never touches a Python tool pays nothing extra.

7
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:38,332
The contract matches the desktop's python underscore run exactly: run the tool's code, call the named function with the JSON arguments, hand back json dot dumps of the result.

8
00:00:38,332 --> 00:00:43,000
Same shape in, same shape out — so nothing above this layer, the tool editor or the model, has to know which one it's talking to.

9
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:46,566
Let's reuse the tool from episode 24 — the one that calls a currency API with urllib.

10
00:00:46,566 --> 00:00:49,671
Same code, same save-and-test flow, but this time it's Pyodide underneath.

11
00:00:49,671 --> 00:00:55,000
Watch it run: real network fetch for the API, but no round-trip to any Python process on this machine, because there isn't one.

12
00:00:55,000 --> 00:01:02,006
Same gotcha as desktop's schema auto-detection, ported over: pyodide dot ts also reimplements python underscore schema, inferring parameters from the function's signature and docstring inside Pyodide itself.

13
00:01:02,006 --> 00:01:06,000
Convenient, still worth checking — a wrong inferred type still means the model calls with the wrong shape of argument.

14
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:07,348
Python now runs with no server.

15
00:01:07,348 --> 00:01:13,000
Files still need somewhere to live, and a terminal to work in — next up, the sandboxed workspace and the shell built on top of it.

